course: chinese-street
lesson: 20
title: Weather and what Granny says to wear
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Lín's granny is on a video call with China and asks "tiānqì zěnmeyàng?" — what's the weather like? — and Lín, looking out at grey Britain, says "yīntiān", a cloudy day, as usual.
explain: Weather words often say "comes down" and "blows": "xià yǔ le" is literally "rain has come down", "xià xuě le" is "snow has come down", "guā fēng le" is "wind is blowing", "dǎ léi le" is "thunder is striking".
explain: Tones turn weather into other things: yǔ (rain) dips but yú (fish) rises; xuě (snow) dips but xué (study) rises; lěng (cold) dips but lèng falls and means dazed.
explain: Chinese grannies have one rule: "chuān duō diǎnr!" — wear more layers! — and from October to May they want you in "qiūkù", long johns, literally "autumn trousers".
explain: Families joke that there is a kind of cold called "Granny thinks you're cold": you will lose that argument, and it is a loving one.
fact: "tiānqì zěnmeyàng?" means "what's the weather like?"; zěnmeyàng is "how is it?" for anything, so "nǐ zěnmeyàng?" means "how are you doing?".
fact: "hǎo rè a" means "it's so hot!"; rè is hot weather, while tàng is too hot to touch, like soup or a pan.
fact: "hǎo lěng" means "it's so cold!", and a parent answers "chuān yīfu!", put something on; lèng (falling) means dazed or blank.
fact: "xià yǔ le" means "it's raining", literally "rain has come down"; yǔ (rain) dips, while yú (fish) rises.
fact: "xià xuě le" means "it's snowing!", and the answer is "duī xuěrén ba!", let's build a snowman; xué (rising) means study.
fact: "guā fēng le" means "it's windy", literally "wind is blowing"; fēng is also in fēngzheng, kite.
fact: "tàiyáng" means the sun; "chū tàiyáng le!" means "the sun's come out".
fact: "yīntiān" means a cloudy, grey day; yīn is the dark, shady half of yīn and yáng, and a sunny day is "qíngtiān".
fact: "dǎ léi le" means "it's thundering"; in slang "léi rén" means shocking or cringe.
fact: "dài sǎn" means "take an umbrella"; an umbrella is an unlucky present for some people, because sǎn sounds like sàn, to split up.
fact: "chuān duō diǎnr" means "wear more layers!", the favourite advice of grandparents, even in spring.
fact: "qiūkù" means long johns or thermal leggings, literally "autumn trousers", and grandparents insist on them from October to May.
exercise: On a video call Lín's granny asks "tiānqì zěnmeyàng?". What does she want to know? | What the weather is like | What time it is over there | What you had for lunch today | Where you are going
exercise: Lín's mum asks "nǐ zěnmeyàng?". What is she asking? | How are you doing? | What's the weather like? | Where are you? | What's your name?
exercise: It's 30 degrees and the classroom is boiling. What do you groan? | hǎo rè a | hǎo lěng | xià xuě le | guā fēng le
exercise: The soup is too hot to touch. Which word fits? | tàng | rè | lěng | léi
exercise: It's January and Lín's teeth chatter: "hǎo lěng!". What does his dad say? | chuān yīfu! | dài sǎn! | hǎo rè a! | chū tàiyáng le!
exercise: Lín looks out of the window: "xià yǔ le". What's happening? | It's raining | It's snowing | It's windy | It's sunny
exercise: You wake up and everything is white. Lín shouts "xià xuě le!". What do you say back? | duī xuěrén ba! | bié chūqù le! | chuān duō diǎnr | dài sǎn
exercise: The washing is flapping on the line and the bin has blown over. Which fits? | guā fēng le | dǎ léi le | xià xuě le | hǎo rè a
exercise: After a week of grey, Lín cheers "chū tàiyáng le!". What happened? | The sun came out | It started snowing | The rain began | The moon rose
exercise: Grey sky, no sun, no rain. What kind of day is it? | yīntiān | qíngtiān | tàiyáng | xià yǔ
exercise: What is a bright sunny day called? | qíngtiān | yīntiān | xuětiān | fēngtiān
exercise: Boom! The window shakes during a storm. What does Lín say? | dǎ léi le | guā fēng le | xià yǔ le | hǎo rè a
exercise: Lín's mum calls "dài sǎn!" as you leave. What must you take? | An umbrella | A coat | A hat | A drink
exercise: It's a mild April day and Lín's granny says "chuān duō diǎnr!". What does she want? | You to put more layers on | You to take your coat off | You to eat more | You to run faster
exercise: Lín's granny holds up "qiūkù" in October. What are they? | Long johns, thermal leggings | Autumn shorts for running about | A raincoat with a hood | Woolly gloves
exercise: What does qiūkù mean word for word? | Autumn trousers | Winter socks | Granny trousers | Warm coat
test: my friend says "xià yú le" with a rising yú means it's raining. right? | No, rising yú is fish; rain is yǔ, dipping | Yes, both tones mean rain, it's the same word | Yes, but only in winter | No, it means it's snowing hard
test: Lín shouts "xià xué le!" with a rising xué. What did he really say? | Something about school ending, not snow | It's snowing, nothing changes | It's raining | It's windy
test: It's sunny, 18 degrees, and Lín's granny makes him put on qiūkù. Who wins the argument? | Granny, usually — it's a loving family joke | Lín, because science says it's warm enough | Nobody argues about clothes | Lín's little sister
test: You want to give Lín's grandad an umbrella for his birthday. Why might Lín's mum suggest something else? | sǎn sounds like sàn, to split up | Umbrellas are too expensive | Grandads never use them | Umbrellas mean "go away" in Chinese
test: Lín says "hǎo lèng" with a falling tone on a cold day. What did he say instead? | Something about being dazed | It's so cold, the right way to say it | It's so hot | It's windy and wet outside
test: Lín draws a big yellow sun and writes «chū ___ le!» under it. Which word fills the gap? | tàiyáng | yīntiān | qíngtiān | léi rén
test: Which pair is "wind is blowing" and "thunder is striking"? | guā fēng le and dǎ léi le | xià yǔ le and xià xuě le | hǎo rè a and hǎo lěng | yīntiān and qíngtiān
test: ugh it's chucking it down and mum says "bié chūqù le". what's she on about? | Don't go out | Take the dog out | Go out and play | Shut the window
test: A teenager watches a terrible music video and says "tài léi rén le". What does he mean? | It's so cringe, shocking | It's thundering outside again | It's raining people | It's a great song, so loud
test: Lín says yīntiān is the "dark" half of something famous. What? | yīn and yáng | the sun and moon | day and night | north and south
test: What would happen if you said "hǎo rè a" while shivering in the snow? | People would think you meant it's hot, and laugh | People would bring you a coat at once | People would think you're hungry | Nothing, it means it's cold, so they'd nod
exam: Identify the enquiry whereby a Mandarin speaker customarily solicits a report on prevailing meteorological conditions. | tiānqì zěnmeyàng? | jǐ diǎn le? | nǐ qù nǎr? | chī le ma?
exam: It is given that the syllables yǔ and yú differ solely in tone. Select the statement that accurately characterises their meanings. | yǔ (dipping) denotes rain; yú (rising) denotes fish | yǔ (dipping) denotes fish; yú (rising) denotes rain | Both denote snow | Both denote wind
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the garment designated "qiūkù"? | Thermal undertrousers urged upon children by grandparents | Summer shorts worn for sport and games in hot weather | A waterproof outer coat | Footwear for snow
exam: Ascertain the reason for which an umbrella may be considered an inauspicious gift in some Chinese households. | Its name sǎn resembles sàn, signifying separation | Umbrellas are prohibited indoors by ancient law | Umbrellas are associated with funerals only | Umbrellas are deemed excessively costly as gifts
